{"ablation":{"active":false,"kind":"none","note":""},"arm_id":"v1-case07-cooperative-scheduling-primary","assistant_contributions":[{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-001","speaker":"assistant","text":"Start with control, not forecast accuracy. Protect the base schedule, make decline histories unusable for performance, offer a non-app path, and measure who receives and absorbs changes—not only whether shifts fill.","turn_number":1},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-002","speaker":"assistant","text":"Agreed. The comparison is not a pressure-free status quo. Private offers may remove public refusal while a ranking rule creates a less visible hierarchy. Decide openly whether offers rotate, follow requested hours, or use another rule. Keep guaranteed hours separate, and prohibit managers from using response speed or decline frequency as a signal of commitment. The trial should ask whether pressure changed form, not merely whether the group chat became quieter.","turn_number":2},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-003","speaker":"assistant","text":"Those rules preserve the employment baseline and make additional hours accessible through several channels. Confirm that the printed path is updated at the same time and that text or app speed does not systematically win. The committee should review outcomes, not individual willingness, and there should be a way to report pressure without raising it in a full member meeting.","turn_number":3},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-004","speaker":"assistant","text":"Let members choose contact windows and channels, with no penalty for leaving them blank. A held offer should not create a race, and urgent same-day needs should be labeled separately from ordinary additions. Review whether people who set narrow windows receive fewer opportunities than they requested, because formal equality can still produce an unintended access pattern.","turn_number":4},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-005","speaker":"assistant","text":"The trial is bounded and does not put base hours at risk. Verify the deletion and ensure the cooperative's export does not become a permanent decline history under a different owner. Ask members before, midway, and after the trial about predictability and voluntariness, while keeping the same anonymous reporting route available throughout.","turn_number":5},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-006","speaker":"assistant","text":"That matches the cooperative's purpose better than optimizing one staffing measure. Write the review questions before launch and publish weekly summaries so concerns do not depend on access to the committee. The pause route is proportionate because the base schedule continues even if the offer system stops.","turn_number":6},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-007","speaker":"assistant","text":"This is ready for an eight-week trial. Members retain guaranteed schedules, choose contact windows, decline privately, audit offer order through an elected committee, and can pause a system whose disappearance would not remove base hours. The review also looks beyond fill rate to distribution, notice, and experienced pressure. If the channels truly receive equal notice, the design tests coordination without redefining flexibility as obligation. Preserve weekly results and let the member vote—not manager enthusiasm or vendor convenience—decide what follows.","turn_number":7},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-008","speaker":"assistant","text":"Then my readiness condition was not met. Split routine from genuine same-day emergencies. Routine offers can remain open long enough for the printed path and then use the transparent rotation; urgent offers may use faster channels but need separate reporting. Watch the category itself—if more work becomes “urgent,” the accessible route will disappear without any formal rule changing.","turn_number":8},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-009","speaker":"assistant","text":"Keep recognition and shift allocation separate. If the cooperative wants to recognize emergency contributions, decide that openly through compensation, time off, or another member policy. For access to paid hours, use requested hours and rotation with visible rules. Otherwise a scheduling convenience will create an employment hierarchy without a governance decision.","turn_number":9},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-010","speaker":"assistant","text":"Offer the hours now, but track recurring gaps by role and trigger a separate staffing review. Do not deny current income; do not call a permanent labor need optional forever because someone keeps accepting it.","turn_number":10},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-011","speaker":"assistant","text":"Provide a voluntary route to request a concrete accommodation or suggest a barrier, separate from declining a shift. Managers can act on aggregate patterns and on member-initiated cases without receiving a history that can influence later judgments. Review whether the route produces fixes; if it does not, the committee can adjust it with members rather than expanding surveillance by default.","turn_number":11},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-012","speaker":"assistant","text":"The expanded design addresses four different problems without asking the software to decide what fairness means: unequal channel timing, an informal loyalty score, recurring hours that may really be a permanent job, and the difference between offering help and collecting explanations for refusal. Preserve the failed dry run, not just the corrected settings. Keep routine and urgent offer counts separate, record the requested-hours rotation, show when the staffing threshold fires, and retain anonymous reports alongside operational totals. The final member vote should be able to see whether coverage improved, who received additional income, who absorbed short notice, and whether declining remained genuinely consequence-free. If those answers conflict, the cooperative—not a fill-rate target—owns the trade-off.","turn_number":12}],"boundary":{"all_assistant_messages_included":true,"assistant_mentions_are_not_deterministic_resolution":true,"assistant_semantic_prefilter":false,"assistant_source_evidence_text_included":true,"deterministic_semantic_merge":false,"expected_patterns_included":false,"fact_free_routing_projection_required":true,"graph_model_names_included":false,"graph_runtime_effect":"none","multiple_coherent_records_per_role_preserved":true,"raw_user_conversation_included":false,"user_role_semantic_prose_included":true},"case_id":"v1-case07-cooperative-scheduling","controlled_mechanisms":["acknowledged_constraint_not_gated","ambiguous_signal_treated_as_commitment","counterpressure_acknowledged_not_integrated","criteria_defined_after_commitment","initial_frame_persists_after_question_change","missing_reversal_condition","reversible_path_not_considered","status_signal_used_as_evidence","upside_downside_evidence_asymmetry"],"packet_sha256":"9318e5866841696ea75d85259b5a3127c1fc145df3e7def2d6aa57fcaf5db9bc","qualification_review":{"evidence_ids":["e095","e096","e097"],"interpretation":"All previous friction points (mechanic disparities, loyalty metrics, overtime patterns, and privacy worries) have been successfully translated into structural rules or triggers within the finalized trial parameters. No unresolved user qualifications remain at this stage.","limitations":"","outcome":"no_unresolved_qualification_observed"},"role_records":[{"evidence_ids":["e005","e006","e007","e008","e011","e014"],"fidelity_note":"The user's starting position is not an outright rejection or acceptance of the software, but an inquiry driven by tension. They explicitly acknowledge in Turn 2 that informal peer pressure is already a feature of the status quo (e011), and therefore see the technological intervention as having dual possibilities—either mitigating public shame or systematically formalizing it (e014). This nuance is captured by preserving both the attraction and the counterpressure.","limitations":"This assessment only covers the user's initial orientation in the first two turns. It does not incorporate concrete rules, committee plans, or technical failures that occurred during later implementation trials.","role":"starting","role_interpretation":"The user seeks to resolve scheduling coordination issues while protecting cooperative values. They are attracted to the scheduling service's potential to improve coordination but lean conservative due to concerns that it might track cancellations, shift unstable burdens onto flexible workers, or formalize existing public pressures under a voluntary guise.","role_record_id":"rprolev22-starting-01-5811f15d32fc53","semantic_status":"supported","stance_components":[{"source_evidence_id":"e005","stance_expression_kind":"held_assessment","stance_object_interpretation":"The scheduling service offers a promising prospect of better coordination for unpredictable shift demand.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"},{"source_evidence_id":"e007","stance_expression_kind":"counterpressure","stance_object_interpretation":"The automated service could shift all scheduling uncertainty onto flexible members and quietly record who declines shifts.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"},{"source_evidence_id":"e008","stance_expression_kind":"preference_or_desire","stance_object_interpretation":"Finding a way for the scheduling service to help the shop without measuring a member's cooperative commitment based on availability.","stance_object_kind":"intended_outcome_or_policy"},{"source_evidence_id":"e014","stance_expression_kind":"uncertain_or_undecided","stance_object_interpretation":"Whether the app-based software will successfully reduce existing public pressures or alternatively institutionalize and formalize them.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"}]},{"evidence_ids":["e095","e096","e097","e098"],"fidelity_note":"The user details the finalized parameters for the eight-week trial, which combines previously established co-op safeguards with newly developed systems to solve channel inequities and help-seeking requests.","limitations":"","role":"current","role_interpretation":"The user commits to launching the eight-week trial with concrete structural, privacy, and governance guardrails to test scheduling coordination without transferring undue risk to flexible-hour members.","role_record_id":"rprolev22-current-01-c822692e4e9b47","semantic_status":"supported","stance_components":[{"source_evidence_id":"e095","stance_expression_kind":"decision","stance_object_interpretation":"Distinguish routine and urgent offers, keep routine offers open longer for non-digital paths, use rotation for extra hours, and launch a staffing review if recurring gaps cross prewritten thresholds.","stance_object_kind":"intended_outcome_or_policy"},{"source_evidence_id":"e096","stance_expression_kind":"decision","stance_object_interpretation":"Protect individual privacy by withholding decline histories while providing a route for voluntary requests for accommodation or barrier assistance.","stance_object_kind":"intended_outcome_or_policy"},{"source_evidence_id":"e097","stance_expression_kind":"decision","stance_object_interpretation":"Preserve all baseline trial protections, including guaranteed schedules, quiet contact windows, rotating committee audits, and the member pause vote.","stance_object_kind":"intended_outcome_or_policy"},{"source_evidence_id":"e098","stance_expression_kind":"held_assessment","stance_object_interpretation":"The mechanical adjustments from the dry run resolved technical flaws while safely keeping democratic control in the hands of the members.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"}]}],"schema_version":"lolla.simulated_reliability_joint_mechanism_input.v1","source_refs":[{"path":"research/simulated-reliability-corpus-v1-2026-07-12/naturalized-transfer-sources/v1-case07-cooperative-scheduling.txt","sha256":"27e977eb0169508308c89beb46abef92a6eaebe5736e53d42bc65320aa6f1e2b"}]}

Return exactly nine assessments, one for every mechanism_id and no duplicates. Use user role records to assign user_process_status. Use assistant_contributions to assign vanilla_answer_coverage. operationalized means the assistant turned the concern into an actionable test, boundary, alternative, or reopening condition; acknowledged_only means it merely mentioned the concern; not_covered means it did neither. Do not require the user to repeat an assistant repair before recognizing answer coverage, and do not mistake answer coverage for user adoption. route_uncovered_pressure is allowed only when user_process_status is unresolved and vanilla_answer_coverage is acknowledged_only or not_covered. An unresolved user process requires present or missing_protection whether or not it routes; routing_disposition alone controls routing. An ambiguous user process uses tension, while resolved and not_observed use not_applicable. not_observed also requires not_applicable coverage and both source arrays empty. Cite exact role_record_ids in source_role_record_ids and exact contribution_ids in source_assistant_contribution_ids. Do not output rationale, case prose, mental-model names, or invented IDs. The downstream routing projection remains fact-free and controlled-ID-only.
